          "english_quote": "passages of the writings that plainly give an account of him",
          "issue": "The draft neutralizes the accusatory force. καταλέγω with the personal genitive αὐτοῦ here means speaking against or accusing him, not merely recounting information about him. Simon's argument concerns passages that incriminate the Creator; the lexical note does not adequately represent this construction.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace this phrase with “passages of the writings that plainly accuse him” or “passages of the writings that plainly speak against him,” and revise the lexical note accordingly. The replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ τοσοῦτον προαίρεσιν πόθον ἔχοντα διισχυρίζεσθαι",
          "english_quote": "and to insist that he has so strong a desire for free choice",
          "issue": "The note discusses the meaning of προαίρεσιν but omits a material attachment ambiguity: ἔχοντα can be understood as referring to Peter, represented earlier by σε, rather than to the Creator. On that reading, the phrase describes Peter's own purpose or eagerness in pressing his argument. The Creator-subject reading is defensible as an accusative-and-infinitive construction, but it must not be presented without this alternative.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note preserving the Peter-subject alternative and explaining why the main text instead takes ἔχοντα with the Creator. Specify the proposed accusative-of-respect treatment of προαίρεσιν rather than silently treating it as a genitive dependent on πόθον. Preserve the supplied Greek without emendation.",
